<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">International Petroleum Company</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1914">1914</a>–<a href="/years/1968">1968</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The International Petroleum Company was a Canadian-registered subsidiary of Standard Oil of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-jersey">New Jersey</a> that controlled the La Brea y Pariñas oilfields in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>. Disputes over its legal title and taxes became a national issue, and a scandal over a 1968 agreement with Fernando Belaúnde's government, including a missing page of the contract, provided a pretext for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-velasco-alvarado">Juan Velasco Alvarado</a>'s coup. Velasco nationalised its holdings days later, in October 1968.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/international-petroleum-company.jpg" alt="International Petroleum Company" width="320" height="227" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ExxonMobil · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Esso_textlogo.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
